.TH XUR.STD.TIME 7 "2026-10-29" "xur 0.1.2" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur.std.time \- timing, durations and sleep
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B use std::time;
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B std::time
provides monotonic timing with
.B Instant
and
.BR Duration ,
plus a sleep function.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B time::now() -> Instant
The current monotonic time.
.TP
.B time::sleep(ms)
.TQ
.B time::sleep(duration)
Pause the current thread for the given milliseconds or Duration.
.TP
.B time::from_secs(n) -> Duration
Create a Duration from seconds (int or float).
.TP
.B time::from_millis(n) -> Duration
Create a Duration from milliseconds.
.SH INSTANT METHODS
.TP
.B elapsed() -> Duration
Time elapsed since the Instant was created.
.SH DURATION METHODS
.TP
.B secs() -> int
Whole seconds.
.TP
.B millis() -> int
Total milliseconds.
.TP
.B micros() -> int
Total microseconds.
.TP
.B nanos() -> int
Total nanoseconds.
.TP
.B as_secs_f64() -> float
Seconds as a floating-point number.
.PP
Duration values print in a human-friendly format: "1.234s", "42ms",
"100\(mcs", or "500ns".
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
use std::time;
use std::io;

fn main() {
    let start = time::now();
    // ... work ...
    let elapsed = start.elapsed();
    io::println("took {}", elapsed);
    io::println("{:.3}s", elapsed.as_secs_f64());
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xur.std (7),
.BR xur.std.datetime (7),
.BR xur.std.thread (7)
